---

<PageLayout title="“A Glimpse of the Circassian Man of Letters Amin Ayyub Samkoug” (Circassia News)">
  <p>
    <a href="/people/amin-samkough/">&larr; Amin Samkough</a> &middot;
    <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/">Research</a>
  </p>
  <hr />

  <section id="details">
    <h2>About this page</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Article</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">«لمحة عن الاديب الشركسي أمين أيوب سمكوغ»</span>
        (“A glimpse of the Circassian man of letters Amin Ayyub Samkoug”)
      </dd>
      <dt>Website</dt>
      <dd>
        <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">أخبار شركيسيا</span> (“Circassia News”), an
        Arabic-language news site
      </dd>
      <dt>Author</dt>
      <dd>None given</dd>
      <dt>Published</dt>
      <dd>
        <time datetime="2016-02-18">18 February 2016</time> (the page shows
        <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">«فبراير 18, 2016»</span>, filed under the
        category “Circassian homeland”)
      </dd>
      <dt>Source</dt>
      <dd>
        <a href={sourceUrl}>circassianews.com</a>
      </dd>
      <dt>Language</dt>
      <dd>Arabic</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      This post is, as far as I can tell, <strong>a copy of the
      <a href={blog}>Circassia Times post</a></strong> of the same date, and so
      for most of its length of the
      <a href={esyria}>2011 eSyria article</a> too. It is also someone else’s
      published work, so I have <strong>not</strong> translated it in full.
      This page records how it compares and lists what to check. Please see the
      Circassia Times page for a summary of the text, a translation of the
      four key passages, and the review notes, all of which apply here.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of
      or that disagrees with another source on this site.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="comparison">
    <h2>How it compares with the Circassia Times post</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The text matches</strong>, section by section: the opening
        paragraph, “His upbringing and birth”, “His academic qualifications”,
        “His life in Paris”, the quotation from “Qabartay”, “His national
        positions” (with the Maluhi quotation) and the friend’s tribute.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The same oddities carry over.</strong> The birth year is given as
        1903 with the same unexplained asterisk, the Paris dates are 1921–1929
        (called “about ten years”), the parliament seat is dated 1931 in the
        opening and 1930 in the body, and the Maluhi quotation has the same
        unexplained asterisk. The stray closing quote after the education
        passage is also there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No credit is given.</strong> The post names neither eSyria nor
        Circassia Times, and gives no references.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No reader comments</strong> appear in the text I could read
        (Circassia Times has one, from 21 February 2016).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>not independent.</strong> A third copy of the eSyria
        text does not confirm anything in it. Every review item on the
        <a href={blog}>Circassia Times page</a> applies here as well.
      </mark>
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: <strong>which came first.</strong> Both posts are dated 18
        February 2016, and I don’t know which was posted first, or whether one
        copied the other or both copied a third source. This page is listed
        after the Circassia Times one only because I found that one first.
      </mark>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="translation-notes">
    <h2>Translation notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        I compared the Arabic text myself and have not had it checked by an
        Arabic reader. The comparison above is of wording and structure, not
        every letter.
      </li>
      <li>
        I read the page as text only, so I can’t say whether the page’s images
        match the portrait on the Circassia Times post. No images are
        reproduced here.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read the full post in Arabic at
      <a href={sourceUrl}>circassianews.com</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

